W: 1. Lay still my fond shepherd and don't you rise yet
W: It's a fine dewy morning and besides, my love, it is wet.
W:
W: 2. Oh let it be wet my love and ever so cold
W: I will rise my fond Floro and away to my fold.
W:
W: 3. Oh no, my bright Floro, it is no such thing
W: It's a bright sun a-shining and the lark is on the wing.
W:
W: 4. Oh the lark in the morning she rises from her nest
W: And she mounts in the air with the dew on her breast
W:
W: 5. And like a pretty ploughboy she'll whistle and sing
W: And at night she will return to her own nest again
W:
W: 6. When the ploughboy has done all he's got for to do
W: He trips down to the meadows where the grass is all cut down.
W:
W: 7. Oh the lark in the morning she rises from her nest
W: And she climbs to the dawn with the dew on her breast
W:
W: 8. And like a pretty ploughboy she'll whistle and sing
W: And at night she will return to her own nest again
%
